//! The devtools window as a ui-app app, in three columns under a top bar: the inspected app's
//! element tree on the left; in the middle a tab for each panel the app's extensions add that
//! the app renders (a silk-ui program's Program panel), the event log, the frame timeline with
//! the frame stats, the accessibility audit, then the extensions' data panels; on the right the
//! inspector of the selected node: its Design (fields for the common properties, edited in
//! place), its Declarations (the node view, edited by typing declarations, and its box model)
//! and the Edits (style edits and comments, kept in the app for an agent to carry into the
//! code). It talks to the app only through the inspector protocol (the same requests
//! ui-inspect-cli and ui-inspect-mcp send), over a [`Link`] the host provides. The look is
//! `theme.silk`'s.
//!
//! The columns keep their designed widths in a wide window and narrow with it; below
//! [`STACKED_BELOW`] the inspector shares the middle column as a tab of its own (see
//! [`columnsFor`]). Each column scrolls on its own.
//!
//! An extension panel shows its data with a generic view of rows, or, when the panel has a
//! view, what the app renders for it: the app's mutation batches, replayed by the host under the
//! panel's box (see `remote.silk`). Devtools know no extension by name.
//!
//! Hovering a row highlights its node in the app's window; selecting a row inspects that node
//! and keeps it highlighted; Pick selects the node pressed in the app's window instead. While
//! live (the default), the devtools subscribe to the app's topics and read again only what a
//! notification says changed (the host sends [`Message.Pull`] when one comes); while the app
//! cannot be reached, they try again every [`RETRY_INTERVAL`] seconds. Keys: ArrowDown and
//! ArrowUp move the selection, Escape clears it, F5 refreshes.
//!
//! Style edits go through the app's override layer (`setStyle`): they stay over what the app
//! sets until reset or resolved, and the Styles panel marks edited declarations and the app's
//! that they hide. The declarations field completes property names and values from the css
//! package's tables, and ArrowUp and ArrowDown in it step the last number (Shift by 10, Alt by
//! 0.1), applying it at once.
